<p>The material behind your cabinet doors and carcass decides how well the kitchen copes with heat, steam and spills over the years. In a humid climate like Malaysia's, how a material reacts to moisture matters more than most buyers expect. This guide explains the common options in general terms so you can ask better questions. Exact performance depends on the grade, the glue, the edging and the workmanship, so always ask your supplier for the specification of what you are quoted.</p>

<h2>Cabinet carcass and door board materials</h2>
<div class="guide-table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>What it is</th><th>Generally good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Plywood</td><td>Thin layers of wood veneer glued together, with the grain alternating.</td><td>Strength and screw-holding; carcasses and shelves; areas that may meet some moisture.</td><td>Quality varies a lot between grades and glues. Exposed edges need sealing or edging.</td></tr>
<tr><td>MDF (medium-density fibreboard)</td><td>Wood fibres pressed with resin into a smooth, dense board.</td><td>Smooth surfaces for painted or sprayed doors, and routed profiles.</td><td>Heavy, and can swell if water gets into cut or damaged edges.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Particleboard / chipboard</td><td>Wood chips and resin pressed into board, usually faced with melamine or laminate.</td><td>Lower-cost cabinets in dry areas, such as upper cabinets.</td><td>Generally the most sensitive to water, especially at edges and around the sink. Keep it away from leaks.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Solid wood</td><td>Sawn timber used for frames, doors or whole cabinets.</td><td>A natural look; frames and doors.</td><td>Wood expands and contracts with humidity, so it can move or crack if not well seasoned and finished. Often costs more.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Surface finishes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Melamine:</strong> a resin-impregnated paper fused onto board. A common, practical finish with many colours and woodgrain patterns; edges and corners are the weak points if hit or soaked.</li>
<li><strong>High-pressure laminate (HPL):</strong> a harder, thicker laminate often chosen for worktops and doors that take more wear. Quality and thickness vary by product.</li>
<li><strong>Veneer:</strong> a thin slice of real wood on board, giving real woodgrain. It needs a good protective finish and care around water.</li>
<li><strong>Sprayed paint or lacquer:</strong> a smooth painted look, often on MDF. It can be touched up but may chip or mark if knocked.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminium frames with glass:</strong> used for glass-door cabinets; wipe-clean and moisture-tolerant, but different in look and cost from board doors.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where each material makes most sense</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sink and dishwasher area:</strong> choose the most moisture-tolerant carcass and edging your budget allows, and make sure joints and cut-outs are sealed.</li>
<li><strong>Cooker area:</strong> heat and grease matter; pick finishes that are easy to wipe. See our guide to <a href="/tips-for-cleaning-kitchen-cabinets/">cleaning kitchen cabinets</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Upper cabinets and dry storage:</strong> lower-risk areas where lower-cost boards can be reasonable if the kitchen is well ventilated.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Questions to ask your supplier</h2>
<ol>
<li>What exact board is used for the carcass, doors and shelves, and what thickness?</li>
<li>How are the edges sealed or edged, particularly near the sink?</li>
<li>What finish is on the doors, and how is it repaired if damaged?</li>
<li>Which hinges and drawer runners are included, and what is their rating or brand?</li>
<li>What warranty applies, and what does it exclude (for example water damage from leaks)?</li>
</ol>
<p>Written answers to these questions make quotations from different suppliers much easier to compare.</p>

